$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0}\dfrac{e^x-\cos{x}}{4\sin{x}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0}\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}[e^x-\cos{x}]}{\dfrac{d}{dx}[4\sin(x)]}\qquad\gray{\text{L’Hôpital’s rule}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0}\dfrac{e^x+\sin(x)}{4\cos(x)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{e^{(0)}+\sin(0)}{4\cos(0)}\qquad\gray{\text{Substitution}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac14
\end{align}$

Note that we were only able to use L’Hôpital’s rule because the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0}\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}[e^x-\cos{x}]}{\dfrac{d}{dx}[4\sin(x)]}$ actually exists.
